A||N||4||SloPonti||Rear Wheel Bearing replacment questions!||||5||
Z||000000||SloPonti||05-31-2000||12:29 AM||||tommorrow night i am replacing the rear pasengers side wheel bearing! some of you know that i was having a problem with a loud noise from the rear end after i got new tires and a alignment done! well i have since got different tires thinking it would change and it did not and i also had the shop recheck the alignment and it is correct but i have found out that the noise is coming from the rear passenger tire area so tomorrow night i am going to replace the wheel bearing-any hints or suggestions or tips? thanks guys and i will let you know if it gets rid of the noise or not!<BR>Rob||||reg||5
Z||000001||Oreif||05-31-2000||02:43 AM||||You need a 30mm deep well socket and a Big breaker bar. To keep the hub from spining you can put a socket thru the cut-out onto the mounting bolt to break the center nut loose. Do not use the same center nut you took off. The new bearing hub should come with a new nut.  To remove the bearing off the axle, just tap the center of the axle with a hammer and pull the hub off. You will also notice that there is no listing in ANY Fiero service manual about how to tighten the center nut if you have aluminum wheels! They all say to torque the nut to 74ftlbs then put on the steel wheels, lower car and torque to 200ftlbs. The trick is to put a jackstand unter the control arm and lower car so the car is stable and torque it using a socket to keep hub from spinning. The scraping noise you'll hear when you are done is the brake dust shield. As soon as you remove it, it seems to bend (???) When re-assembling put rotor on and check the clearence so you won't have the noise.  ||||reg||1
Z||000002||bHooper||05-31-2000||11:04 AM||||Let me know how this goes.  I am also having some strange sounds, coming from my right rear, and have decided that it is a bearing problem.<P>hoop||||reg||1
Z||000003||theogre||05-31-2000||11:10 AM||||take the axle nut off before you remove the wheel. (or mount the spare if you have AL wheels and don't want to risk breaking/loosing the center cap.) It's much easier to get that nut off with the car on the ground and the brakes set. (Have someone sit and hold the peddle if need be)<P>you need a good 3 foot bar/pipe to get it loose. 1/2 drive is a must. You can take it off with an air wrench. DO NOT put it on with one. You must hold the brake if you use an air wrench to prevent damaging the CV joints. You'll need the same long bar/pipe to tighten it.<P>It is a lock nut. it should be replaced once it's removed. expect to use a wrench all the way off and on. WARNING: This thing get's HOT.||||reg||1
Z||000004||Raydar||05-31-2000||08:38 PM||||Rob,<BR>I was pleasantly surprised when I replaced mine. Was one of the easier things that I have ever done to my Fiero. Had to buy that expensive d@%# socket, though.||||reg||1
